<main id="main">
<h1>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) &mdash; The Semantic Retrieval Engine</h1>
<div class="hero">
  <p class="subtitle">The Semantic Retrieval Engine</p>
  <p>Multi-format chunking, hybrid retrieval (keyword + semantic + graph), hierarchical summarization via RAPTOR, entity resolution, and background task execution.</p>
</div>

<!-- WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS — explains purpose and design rationale -->
<div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;padding:1.4rem 1.6rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
<h3 style="margin-top:0;color:var(--accent);">&#127919; Why This Module Exists</h3>
<p>The RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) module is the semantic retrieval engine that powers every knowledge-based operation in RAGFlow. Without this module, the system would have no way to intelligently search documents, reason about relationships, or synthesize multi-source answers. The RAG module orchestrates a sophisticated pipeline: from multi-format document chunking (14 document-type handlers), through hybrid keyword-semantic retrieval with entity graph reasoning, to hierarchical summarization via RAPTOR. It balances precision (BM25 keyword matching), recall (dense semantic embeddings), and graph-based reasoning to surface the most relevant context. This module exists separately because retrieval is a fundamentally different concern from indexing, generation, and orchestration—each with its own performance bottlenecks and optimization strategies. The entire system's quality depends on RAG relevance; a weak retrieval engine cannot be fixed by a better LLM. — Replace with 150+ words explaining what problem this module solves, why it exists as a separate module (not merged into something else), and what the system would look like without it. Use narrative voice: "Without this module, every component would need to..."</p>
<details style="margin-top:.8rem;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent2);font-weight:600;">&#128161; Real-world analogy</summary>
<p style="margin-top:.5rem;padding-left:1rem;border-left:3px solid var(--accent3);color:var(--text-muted);">Imagine a massive library where you're the retrieval specialist. You understand that books (documents) have multiple ways to be found: by title (keyword search), by theme and narrative flow (semantic search), and by how different ideas in the library relate to each other (entity graphs). When someone asks a question, you don't just hand them one book—you intelligently combine keyword lookups with semantic similarity to find the most relevant sections, then trace relationships between concepts to enrich the answer. That's what RAG does: it's the librarian that transforms vague queries into precise, contextual information. — A vivid metaphor mapping this module's core concept to everyday life. 3-5 sentences.</p>
</details>
</div>

