<!-- END-TO-END NARRATED WALKTHROUGH -->
<div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;padding:1.4rem 1.6rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
<h3 style="margin-top:0;color:var(--accent);">&#128694; Step-by-Step Narrated Walkthrough</h3>
<p style="color:var(--text-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;">Follow a single <strong>user query to retrieval result</strong> as it flows through the entire RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) pipeline:</p>
<ol style="line-height:2;">
<ol style="line-height:2;">
        <li><strong>Query arrives at RAG module</strong> — User query enters the pipeline. The system determines if it's a search, summarization, or chat request. Typical latency: <1ms.</li>
        <li><strong>Query encoding</strong> — The query is tokenized and embedded using a dense embedding model (e.g., BGE-large). Simultaneously, keyword analysis (BM25 tokenization) prepares it for hybrid retrieval. Latency: ~50-200ms depending on model.</li>
        <li><strong>BM25 keyword search</strong> — Traditional TF-IDF based search against inverted index. Fast O(log n) lookup. Returns top-k keywords matches. Latency: ~5-20ms.</li>
        <li><strong>Dense semantic search</strong> — Vector similarity search against embedding indices (Elasticsearch, Infinity, ONNX). Returns semantically similar chunks even with different keywords. Latency: ~20-100ms depending on index size.</li>
        <li><strong>Entity graph traversal</strong> — If entity relationships exist, traverse the knowledge graph for related concepts and entities. Adds tertiary ranking signal. Latency: ~10-50ms.</li>
        <li><strong>Result fusion</strong> — Combine BM25 scores (40%) + dense scores (60%) + entity signals. Re-rank by combined score. This is where RAG's "hybrid" advantage emerges. Latency: <5ms.</li>
        <li><strong>Deduplication & filtering</strong> — Remove duplicate chunks, filter by confidence threshold, apply any ACL rules. Latency: <5ms.</li>
        <li><strong>Context window preparation</strong> — Truncate or expand results to fit LLM context window. Preserve order and inter-chunk relationships. Latency: <5ms.</li>
        <li><strong>Optional: hierarchical summarization</strong> — If context is too large, use RAPTOR (Recursive Abstractive Summarization over Tree Structures) to compress while preserving key facts. This involves clustering chunks, LLM summarization, and building a summary tree. Latency: ~1-5 seconds (only when needed).</li>
        <li><strong>Final result to LLM</strong> — The combined ranked context list is passed to the generation module. The LLM uses this context to synthesize an answer. RAG's job is complete; now generation takes over. Latency: <1ms for RAG handoff.</li>
      </ol>
</div>

